import Layout from '../layouts/BlogPost.astro';
---
<Layout
	title="About Me"
	description="Watercolor artist and workshop teacher based in Irapuato, Guanajuato, Mexico."
	pubDate={new Date('August 10 2026')}
	heroImage="/blog-placeholder-about.jpg"
>
	<p>
		I was born in Mexico City in 1958, and since 2010 I've made my home in
		Irapuato, Guanajuato. My path to watercolor wasn't a straight line &mdash;
		it grew out of a broader life in the arts, one shaped first by
		architecture and later by a deep, ongoing commitment to painting,
		research, and teaching.
	</p>

	<h2>Education</h2>
	<p>
		I studied architecture at the Universidad Nacional Autónoma de México,
		and Fine Arts at the Escuela de Pintura, Escultura y Grabado "La
		Esmeralda" and at the Escuela de Artes Plásticas of the Universidad de
		las Américas Puebla. I later pursued a Master's in Heritage Research at
		UIA Puebla, and as part of my thesis research completed an academic
		residency in the Conservation department of the Museo Nacional Centro
		de Arte Reina Sofía in Madrid, Spain. I'm currently completing a
		Doctorate in Art at the Universidad de Guanajuato.
	</p>

	<h2>Watercolor Practice</h2>
	<p>
		I've practiced watercolor painting since 1980, studying under Arq.
		René Capdevielle, Arq. Vicente Mendiola, and Mtro. Alfredo Guati Rojo.
		In 1988 and 1989 I received the Tlacuilo trophy, awarded by the
		Sociedad Mexicana de Acuarelistas and the Museo Nacional de la
		Acuarela, and in 1998 the acquisition prize at the "Color, Agua y
		Papel" watercolor competition sponsored by SunChemical.
	</p>
	<p>
		Over the years I've also taken part in workshops with master
		watercolorists including Andy Evansen, Janine Gallizia, Pedro Cano,
		Marc Folly, Amit Kapoor, Alvaro Castagnet, and Prafull Sawant, among
		others &mdash; experiences that continue to shape how I paint and how
		I teach.
	</p>

	<h2>Exhibitions</h2>
	<p>
		I'm a member of the Sociedad Mexicana de Acuarelistas and of IWS
		México, and have taken part in group exhibitions at both the national
		and international level with these organizations. I've also held solo
		exhibitions in Mexico City in 1999 and in the city of Puebla in 2002.
	</p>

	<h2>Publications & Collections</h2>
	<p>
		My work has been published in <em>The Art of Watercolor</em>, issue
		n° 55, edited and published in France, and is part of the permanent
		collections of the Museo Nacional de la Acuarela, the Museo de la
		Acuarela del Estado de México, the Colección de Periódicos Síntesis in
		Puebla, and the Walter Anderson Museum of Art in Ocean Springs,
		Mississippi, USA.
	</p>

	<h2>Teaching</h2>
	<p>
		Today, alongside my own painting practice, I teach watercolor
		workshops for artists at every level &mdash; from complete beginners
		looking to lose their fear of the brush, to more advanced painters
		refining their technique and finding their own voice. I bring
		everything I've learned from decades of practice, study, and time
		spent with master painters directly into the studio, in a way that's
		hands-on, patient, and grounded in real technique.
	</p>
</Layout>
